1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:09,730
Like many historical films, Amadeus is far from a faithful account of what is known about the period and the people that it portrays.
和许多历史片一样，就其所描绘的时代和人物而言，《莫扎特传》远不是对已知史实的忠实记述。

2
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:20,280
Events are exaggerated, condensed and simplified, and the complexity of real characters is reduced to suit the needs of a dramatic contrast between good and evil.
片中的事件经过了夸大、压缩和简化，真实人物的复杂性也被削减，以满足善恶之间戏剧性对比的需要。

3
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:28,220
Such historical liberties are often bemoaned by experts, but few have seemed to mind the wayward story points of Amadeus.
专家们常常抱怨这类对历史的随意改动，但对于《莫扎特传》中那些背离史实的情节，似乎很少有人介意。

4
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:36,960
This is no doubt partly attributable to the film’s high entertainment value: it is an unusually lively and funny historical film.
这无疑在一定程度上要归功于影片娱乐价值很高：它是一部格外活泼、风趣的历史片。

5
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:49,950
It revels in the boyish humor and high spirits of its main character, the composer Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart (1756–1791), played with jubilant gusto by Tom Hulce.
影片的主人公是作曲家沃尔夫冈·阿马德乌斯·莫扎特（1756—1791），由汤姆·赫尔斯以欢欣饱满的热情饰演；影片尽情展现了他孩子气的幽默和兴高采烈的劲头。

6
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:54,470
But the film’s appeal is also attributable to Mozart’s music.
不过，影片的魅力也要归功于莫扎特的音乐。

7
00:00:55,270 --> 00:01:05,170
The composer’s vulgar hijinks serve as a contrast to the transcendent beauty of his music, beautifully performed on the soundtrack by the Academy of St Martin in the Fields.
这位作曲家粗俗的狂欢作乐，与他音乐的超凡之美形成了对比；在影片配乐中，田野圣马丁学院乐团把这些音乐演奏得十分优美。

8
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:11,930
Thus, even the most stringent historical purists couldn’t help but find something to enjoy in Amadeus.
因此，即便是对史实要求最严苛、最不容走样的人，也不禁会在《莫扎特传》中找到值得欣赏之处。

9
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:19,770
For all its liberties, the story is actually based on a real rumor that circulated in Vienna in the 1820s.
尽管有种种随意改动，这个故事其实取材于19世纪20年代在维也纳流传的一则真实传闻。

10
00:01:20,570 --> 00:01:31,320
While gravely ill, the rival composer Antonio Salieri (1750–1825) confessed he had murdered Mozart decades earlier by poisoning him.
莫扎特的竞争对手、作曲家安东尼奥·萨列里（1750—1825）在病重时供认，自己几十年前下毒谋杀了莫扎特。

11
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:42,770
Salieri was suffering from dementia at the time of this confession, and he later withdrew it, but some—including Mozart’s widow Constanze—chose to believe the claim.
萨列里作出这番供认时正患有痴呆症，后来他又撤回了供认，但仍有一些人——包括莫扎特的遗孀康斯坦策——选择相信这一说法。

12
00:01:43,570 --> 00:02:01,440
More than 150 years later, the English playwright Peter Shaffer based the story of Amadeus not just on Salieri’s confession but also on the idea that Salieri had suffered from a deep and bitter jealousy of Mozart throughout the ten years that they both lived and worked as composers in Vienna.
150多年后，英国剧作家彼得·谢弗创作《莫扎特传》的故事时，不仅以萨列里的供认为依据，还依据了这样一种说法：在两人同为作曲家、在维也纳生活和工作的那十年里，萨列里始终对莫扎特怀着深深的妒恨，饱受其折磨。

13
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:15,180
In the fun-loving Mozart, the story goes, Salieri saw a true genius—one who made his own talent and accomplishments appear mediocre—and this drove him on a vendetta that ultimately culminated in murder.
按照故事的说法，萨列里在爱玩爱闹的莫扎特身上看到了一位真正的天才——这位天才让萨列里自己的才华和成就都显得平庸——这驱使他走上了一条积怨复仇之路，最终酿成了谋杀。

14
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:22,010
Shaffer’s story makes for great drama, but it is, of course, biased against Salieri.
谢弗的故事造就了一出精彩的戏剧，但它当然对萨列里抱有偏见。

15
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:29,310
In fact, at the time, Salieri was regarded as the more accomplished musician and composer.
事实上，在当时，萨列里被视为两人中更有造诣的音乐家和作曲家。

16
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:39,230
From the 1770s through the 1790s, he composed dozens of operas, many of them proving popular and considered innovative.
从18世纪70年代到90年代，他创作了几十部歌剧，事实证明其中许多部都很受欢迎，而且被认为富有新意。

17
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:49,680
A mark of his prominence was his appointment to the influential post of Kapellmeister, or musical director, to the court of Holy Roman Emperor Joseph II.
他地位显赫的一个标志，是被任命为神圣罗马帝国皇帝约瑟夫二世宫廷的乐长（即音乐总监），这是一个颇具影响力的职位。

18
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:56,900
Salieri was also a teacher whose pupils included Ludwig van Beethoven, Franz Liszt and Franz Schubert.
萨列里还是一位老师，他的学生包括路德维希·凡·贝多芬、弗朗茨·李斯特和弗朗茨·舒伯特。

19
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:08,720
In his private life, he may not have had Mozart’s exuberance but nor was he the lonely and celibate man played with such convincing severity by F. Murray Abraham in Amadeus.
在私生活中，他或许没有莫扎特那样的旺盛活力，但他也不是《莫扎特传》里那个孤独、独身禁欲的男人；F·默里·亚伯拉罕把这个人物演得如此冷峻，如此令人信服。

20
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:14,850
Salieri was married at the time he knew Mozart, and he fathered no fewer than eight children.
萨列里与莫扎特相识期间已经结婚，而且他育有不下八个子女。

21
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:25,950
If Salieri had little reason to fear or resent Mozart’s success, there was naturally a degree of rivalry between two men working in the same profession and in the same city.
虽说萨列里几乎没有理由畏惧或怨恨莫扎特的成功，但同在一行、同在一城的两个人之间，自然会存在一定程度的竞争。

22
00:03:26,750 --> 00:03:38,270
Salieri (born near Verona) and Mozart (born in Salzburg) belonged to separate musical groups, and Italian and German opera fell into and out of favor during this period.
萨列里（生于维罗纳附近）和莫扎特（生于萨尔茨堡）分属不同的音乐圈子，而在这一时期，意大利歌剧和德国歌剧时而受宠、时而失宠。

23
00:03:39,070 --> 00:03:48,450
The composers were therefore vying for work, including the prestigious post of musical tutor to the Princess of Württemberg, which Salieri successfully attained.
因此，两位作曲家一直在争夺工作机会，其中包括声望很高的符腾堡公主音乐教师一职，萨列里成功获得了这一职位。

24
00:03:49,250 --> 00:03:56,740
As composers, they saw their operas debut side by side, yet there is little evidence of any animosity between them.
两人同为作曲家，各自的歌剧曾同台首演，然而几乎没有证据表明他们之间存在任何敌意。

25
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:07,020
Mozart did complain in a letter to his father that Joseph II favored Salieri over all other composers, but that observation was an accurate one.
莫扎特确实曾在写给父亲的信中抱怨，约瑟夫二世偏爱萨列里胜过其他所有作曲家，不过这一看法倒是准确的。

26
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:19,810
Both Mozart and his father suspected that, behind the scenes, Salieri tried to undermine Mozart’s success, but these were hardly unusual suspicions in a field so reliant on patronage.
莫扎特和他父亲都怀疑萨列里在幕后企图阻挠莫扎特取得成功，但在一个如此依赖赞助的领域，这类怀疑几乎算不上稀奇。

27
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:26,630
In public, fellow composers reported that Mozart and Salieri were friendly with one another.
据同行作曲家所述，莫扎特和萨列里在公开场合彼此友好。

28
00:04:27,430 --> 00:04:35,620
Shortly after the premiere of Mozart’s The Magic Flute, Salieri attended a performance with Mozart, and applauded warmly and vigorously.
莫扎特的《魔笛》首演后不久，萨列里和莫扎特一起观看了该剧的一场演出，并热情而卖力地鼓掌。

29
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:50,780
Thus, any ill feeling between Mozart and Salieri was borne by the former rather than the latter—contrary to what is strongly depicted in Amadeus—and it stemmed from Salieri’s status and success rather than his perceived mediocrity.
因此，莫扎特与萨列里之间若有任何怨恨，心怀怨恨的也是前者而不是后者——这与《莫扎特传》极力描绘的情形相反；而且这种怨恨源于萨列里的地位和成就，而不是他所谓的平庸。

30
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:56,560
Mozart’s resentments were those of a younger man struggling for position in the world.
莫扎特的怨恨，是年轻后辈的怨恨：他正为在世上谋得一席之地而奋力打拼。

31
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:02,940
Although he had talent, he spent many years struggling to find a suitable post or patronage.
他虽有才华，却花了许多年苦苦寻找合适的职位或赞助。

32
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:13,120
He and his father travelled widely during his youth, seeking a distinguished appointment but finding mainly low pay and occasionally humiliating circumstances.
他年少时与父亲四处游历，想谋求一个显要的职位，得到的却主要是微薄的报酬，偶尔还会陷入令人屈辱的处境。

33
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:23,410
It was in the period after 1781, when Mozart defied his father and decided to live and work independently in Vienna, that his career flourished.
1781年，莫扎特违抗父命，决定在维也纳独立生活和工作；正是在此后的那段时期，他的事业蓬勃发展起来。

34
00:05:24,210 --> 00:05:39,500
In the space of ten years, he found great success with the operas The Abduction from the Seraglio (1782), The Marriage of Figaro (1786), Don Giovanni (1787) and The Magic Flute (1791).
在十年的时间里，他凭借歌剧《后宫诱逃》（1782）、《费加罗的婚礼》（1786）、《唐璜》（1787）和《魔笛》（1791）取得了巨大成功。

35
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:50,380
These were composed alongside his piano concertos, symphonies and chamber music, and together with his work as a performer and teacher, his success brought a high income.
他在创作这些歌剧的同时，还创作了钢琴协奏曲、交响曲和室内乐；创作上的成功，再加上他作为演奏家和教师的工作，为他带来了丰厚的收入。

36
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:59,280
Mozart’s money troubles were the result of excessive spending, and his volatile temperament, rather than any malicious schemes against him.
莫扎特的经济困难，是他开销过度、性情多变的结果，而不是有人针对他设下了什么恶意阴谋。

37
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:07,630
His death, at the age of 35, was not considered suspicious at the time as he had been ill for weeks with a fever.
他35岁去世，当时人们并不认为他的死有什么可疑之处，因为此前他已经发烧病了好几个星期。

38
00:06:08,430 --> 00:06:16,770
While it is true that he had a commoner’s funeral, in 18th-century Vienna this was not unusual for a man of non-aristocratic standing.
他的葬礼诚然是平民规格，但在18世纪的维也纳，对一个非贵族身份的人来说，这并不罕见。

39
00:06:17,570 --> 00:06:22,670
It certainly was not a mark of his downfall or ignominy, as implied by the film.
这当然也不像影片所暗示的那样，是他没落或蒙受耻辱的标志。

40
00:06:23,470 --> 00:06:30,990
At his death, Mozart was second in stature only to Salieri as Vienna’s most prominent musician and composer.
莫扎特去世时，在维也纳最杰出的音乐家和作曲家中，他的地位仅次于萨列里。

41
00:06:31,790 --> 00:06:47,000
The drama of Amadeus stems not from historical accuracies, but from our contemporary knowledge that Mozart’s music and reputation have survived for centuries—and continued to find new, zesty audiences—while Salieri’s name and work quickly faded.
《莫扎特传》的戏剧性并非来自对史实的准确呈现，而是来自我们今天所知道的这一事实：莫扎特的音乐和声誉流传了几个世纪——而且不断赢得热情洋溢的新听众——而萨列里的名字和作品却很快被人淡忘。

42
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:58,470
Mozart’s secondary status during his own lifetime thus appears unjust and unwarranted, and he is invested with the role of the struggling artist and unappreciated genius.
因此，莫扎特生前屈居次席，就显得既不公正，又毫无道理；他也被赋予了一个角色：苦苦挣扎的艺术家、怀才不遇的天才。

43
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:12,970
This may be shaky history, but the film has enough laughter, conflict, romance and tragedy to please any opera lover, except perhaps for Salieri himself, who undoubtedly would have told the tale in an altogether different key.
这或许是靠不住的历史，但影片的欢笑、冲突、爱情和悲剧足以让任何一位歌剧爱好者满意——也许萨列里本人除外，他无疑会用一种截然不同的调子来讲述这个故事。
